---
import ServicePage from "../../components/ServicePage.astro";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What does an arborist assessment include?",
    a: `The arborist inspects the tree and its surroundings, looks for structural defects, decay, root problems and storm damage, rates the risk, and recommends what to do: leave it, prune it or remove it. A written report is available when you need it for a permit, insurer or HOA.`,
  },
  {
    q: "Why do I need a report to remove a tree?",
    a: `Under Florida Statute 163.045, a homeowner may remove a tree on residential property without a local permit when a certified arborist or licensed landscape architect documents that it poses an unacceptable risk. Keep the documentation; the city can ask for it later.`,
  },
  {
    q: "How do I check that an arborist is certified?",
    a: `Ask for their ISA Certified Arborist number and look it up on the International Society of Arboriculture website. See <a href="/tree-care-guides/what-is-an-arborist/">what an arborist is</a> for what the credential means.`,
  },
  {
    q: "Is an arborist the same as a tree removal crew?",
    a: `No. An arborist assesses and advises on tree health and risk; a removal crew does the physical work. Some professionals do both, and an independent assessment is useful when you want advice that does not depend on selling you a removal.`,
  },
  {
    q: "Who provides the assessment?",
    a: `An independent professional, matched to your request. SF Tree Removal is an online service that passes your request on; we do not perform assessments ourselves.`,
  },
];
const extras = [
  {
    "topper": "The inspection",
    "title": "What a Tree Risk Assessment Looks At",
    "image": "trunk-bucket",
    "html": "<p>An arborist looks at the whole tree and what it could hit. The assessment typically covers:</p><ul><li>the trunk, for cracks, cavities, decay and lean;</li><li>the crown, for dead wood, weak unions and imbalance;</li><li>the roots and soil, for lifted or damaged roots and poor drainage;</li><li>the site, including houses, cars, play areas and power lines in the fall zone.</li></ul><p>The result is a risk rating and a recommendation: monitor, prune, treat, cable, or remove.</p>"
  },
  {
    "topper": "Paperwork",
    "title": "Reports for Permits, HOAs and Insurance",
    "image": "ladder-oak",
    "html": "<p>A written report is the proof a city, an HOA or an insurer asks for. Under Florida Statute 163.045, a homeowner of a detached single-family home can remove a tree without a local permit, fee or replacement when an ISA certified arborist or Florida licensed landscape architect documents an unacceptable risk. The report must follow the ISA tree risk assessment method and be signed.</p><p>Say why you need the report when you send the request, so it is written to meet that purpose. See our <a href=\"/tree-removal-permits/\">permit guide</a>.</p>"
  },
  {
    "topper": "Trees worth keeping",
    "title": "Valuable and Older Trees",
    "image": "canopy-climber",
    "html": "<p>A big oak, banyan or mahogany can be worth far more standing than removed. An arborist can recommend pruning, cabling, root care or treatment to extend its life, and advise on protecting it during construction.</p><p>If a tree is on the edge, an independent opinion can save you the cost of a removal you did not need, or confirm that removal is the right call.</p>"
  }
];
---

<ServicePage
  title="Arborist Assessments in South Florida | Tree Risk Reports"
  description="Request a tree health or risk assessment from an independent local arborist in South Florida, including reports for permits, HOAs and insurance."
  service={{
    name: "Arborist Assessment Requests",
    serviceType: "Arborist assessment and tree risk report matching",
    description: "Requests for tree health and risk assessments passed to independent local arborists in South Florida.",
  }}
  badge="ARBORIST ASSESSMENTS"
  h1="Arborist Assessments in South Florida"
  lead="Not sure a tree has to come down? Request an assessment from an independent local arborist."
  intro={[
    "A tree can look fine and still be a risk, or look alarming and be perfectly safe. An arborist can tell the difference, and a written report gives you proof when a city, an HOA or an insurer asks.",
    "Describe the tree in the <a href=\"/contact-us/\">contact form</a> and choose Arborist Assessment. We pass the request to an independent professional for a free estimate. We are active in the Fort Lauderdale area and adding professionals elsewhere; see <a href=\"/areas/#coverage\">where we are active</a>. We do not perform assessments ourselves.",
  ]}
  coversTitle="When an Assessment Helps"
  covers={[
    { name: "Before removing a tree", note: "A professional second opinion that the tree really is a risk, or that pruning would be enough." },
    { name: "For a permit or the state exemption", note: "Written documentation that a tree presents an unacceptable risk, which can exempt a residential removal from a local permit." },
    { name: "For an HOA or insurer", note: "A report on the condition of a tree, or on storm damage, that you can attach to a request or a claim." },
    { name: "After a storm", note: "A check for hidden damage in trees that look fine: cracks, root lift, weakened limbs." },
    { name: "Before building or landscaping", note: "Advice on protecting valuable trees, or deciding which ones to keep, before work near their roots." },
    { name: "Older or valuable trees", note: "Disease, decay and structural problems are easier to treat early, and some trees are worth saving." },
  ]}
  detailTitle="What to Expect"
  detail={`<p>The arborist looks at the trunk, canopy, root zone and surroundings, notes defects such as cracks, cavities, dead wood, fungus and lean, and rates how likely the tree is to fail and what it could hit. You then get a recommendation: monitor, prune, treat or remove.</p>
<p>If you need paperwork, say so when you send the request. A report for a permit or the Florida exemption has to document the risk specifically, so tell the arborist why you need it. Read more in our guides to <a href="/tree-removal-permits/">tree removal permits</a> and <a href="/tree-care-guides/what-is-an-arborist/">what an arborist is</a>.</p>`}
  faqs={faqs}
  image="arborist-inspect"
  introImage="climber-rope"
  introTitle="Understand a Tree Before You Decide"
  extras={extras}
/>
